{
	"version": 5,
	"rows": [
		{
			"id": "tJbhUedM6s8Fcsqri",
			"name": "Unusual Background (Latent Psychic Potential)",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Psychic ability is a random, rare mutation of the human genome that lets its bearer perceive and touch the immaterium (the warp). Sources disagree on exact frequency — Imperial administrative texts and Dark Heresy-line background both describe it as \"rare but not vanishingly so,\" on the order of a handful of latent potentials per hive-sized population, with usably strong psykers far rarer still. Treat any precise ratio as flavour, not census data.\n\nDesign: Required to take Psyker (Talent) or any trait carrying the Warp power modifier below. Basic Set B261 leaves Unusual Background costs to the GM; 10 points reflects that the ability itself is not learned but grants access to an entire framework of further abilities, roughly the going rate other published settings use for \"you may buy from this power list at all.\"",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "tMWW_FyktNxP7N3Sy",
			"name": "Warp (Power Modifier)",
			"local_notes": "Not purchased on its own. Apply \"Warp, -15%\" as a modifier to every advantage built as a psychic ability in this file (Innate Attack, Affliction, Mind Control, Mind Probe, Mind Reading, Telekinesis-based traits, Terror, etc.). It does not apply to the Blank/Pariah gene or the Navigator's third eye, which are inborn mutations, not channelled acts.\n\nLore: Every psychic act in the fiction shares three traits: it can be shut down by a null field (Culexus Assassins, Officio Assassinorum \"blanks,\" null rods, hexagrammic wards, a ship's Geller field extending into a room), it can be sensed by other warp-touched minds (daemons home in on strong psykers; astropaths and Inquisitors can feel a working; the Black Ships find latent talents this way), and using it always risks the warp answering back (see Perils of the Warp, below). Black Library depictions (Eisenhorn, Ravenor, Ahriman, the Horus Heresy Librarius material) and Dark Heresy/Rogue Trader's psychic-power rules agree on all three points.\n\nDesign: Built as two stacked limitations on a single -15% power modifier, shown separately for clarity: (1) Nullified, -10% — the power fails outright in a null field/near a blank, comparable in weight to Basic's own Mana Sensitive limitation (-10%, B112) for a power that simply stops working under specific, uncommon but real battlefield conditions. (2) Detectable, -5% — other warp-sensitive beings (psykers, daemons, the Astronomican-attuned) can sense the use, roughly the mirror of Basic's No Signature enhancement (+20%, B111) which buys detectability away entirely; here only a narrow audience can detect it, so removing that audience's free ability to sense you is worth a smaller -5%. Net: -15%, applied identically to every Warp-tagged ability so their relative costs stay comparable.",
			"tags": [
				"Reference",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 0
		},
		{
			"id": "tfOxB4Cq8HU0pbtyT",
			"name": "Perils of the Warp (Limitation)",
			"local_notes": "Not purchased on its own. Apply \"Perils of the Warp, -10%\" (or the Severe\nvariant, -20%, for rituals, Great Powers, and interstellar workings called\nout below) to every Warp-tagged advantage.\n\nWhenever a character uses an ability carrying this limitation, roll vs\nWill (modified by any Pushing, see below, and by GM-set warp conditions:\n-1 to -5 near a Chaos icon, inside a warp storm, on a ship whose Geller\nfield is failing, etc.). Success: the power works with no ill effect.\nFailure, consult the margin:\n  - Failed by 1-2 (Minor Perils): lose 1 FP; migraine or nosebleed; -1 to\n    the next IQ-based roll this scene.\n  - Failed by 3-5 (Moderate Perils): 1d damage, ignores DR (warp-shock);\n    the power still fizzles; anyone adjacent makes a Fright Check.\n  - Failed by 6-9 (Major Perils): 2d damage, ignores DR; the psyker is\n    stunned (roll vs HT each turn to recover); a minor manifestation\n    appears nearby (cold spot, whispering, flickering shadow) and\n    witnesses roll Fright Check at -2.\n  - Failed by 10+, or an unmodified 18: Catastrophic. A warp rift may\n    open, or a daemon attempts possession: Quick Contest of Will against\n    the daemon (GM sets 12-16 for a minor one) or the psyker is possessed,\n    killed, or reduced to a Chaos Spawn, GM's call. The triggering power\n    still goes off, at full or worse effect, entirely out of its user's\n    control.\n\nPushing: before rolling, a psyker may declare up to 3 pushes. Each push\ngrants +1 to the ability's effective level (an extra die, +1 to an\nAffliction, one more yard of Move, etc., GM's call for the specific power)\nbut applies a cumulative -2 to this Perils roll. Pushing represents\ndrawing more of the warp than the caster's mind and body can safely carry.\n\nLore: Calibrated against Dark Heresy's Psychic Phenomena/Perils of the Warp tables, the best worked example of \"using your gift can doom you,\" and against countless Black Library scenes of psykers burning out, exploding, or being possessed the instant they overreach (Ahriman's own Rubric; Quixos in Eisenhorn; any Chaos-touched sorcerer's Perils rolls in the Black Library Horus Heresy Librarius stories).\n\nDesign: Standard, -10%: comparable in total weight to Basic's Costs Fatigue (-5%, B112, a guaranteed minor cost) stacked with a low-frequency chance of a serious complication similar in spirit to Unreliable (-5% to -10%, B113) — bundled here as one flat roll-and-consult-a-table limitation instead of tracked as separate costs, since the system assumes a single check per activation rather than a metered resource. Severe, -20%: used on rituals, Great Powers, and interstellar workings (Gate of Infinity, Astrotelepathy, Vortex of Doom, Sanctic Daemonology's Banishment and Sanctuary, Librarius's Null Zone) where any serious failure can open a rift regardless of scale — double the standard weight, echoing how Basic's Always On climbs from -10% to -40% (B113) as an effect becomes harder to control.",
			"tags": [
				"Reference",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 0
		},
		{
			"id": "tx6XCYvZwcgAQ5l_j",
			"name": "Psyker",
			"reference": "B89",
			"local_notes": "Applies to: Meditation, Hidden Lore (the Warp), and the Will roll to\nresist Perils of the Warp (see above) — a house extension of Talent's\nnormal \"add to listed skills\" rule, precedented by Magery's own habit of\naiding related rolls beyond pure spellcasting. Reaction bonus: +1/level\nfrom other sanctioned psykers and the Astra Telepathica.\n\nImperial Assignment scale (Adeptus Astra Telepathica / Black Ships),\nweakest to strongest, mapped to Talent level and the practical ceiling on\nany single ability's level/dice bought with it:\n  Iota    — Talent 0 (latent; register but rarely take Psyker traits at all)\n  Zeta    — Talent 1, ability cap ~2\n  Eta     — Talent 1, ability cap ~2\n  Theta   — Talent 2, ability cap ~3\n  Epsilon — Talent 2, ability cap ~4  (typical Wyrdvane conscript)\n  Delta   — Talent 3, ability cap ~5  (typical Sanctioned Primaris Psyker)\n  Gamma   — Talent 4, ability cap ~6\n  Beta    — Talent 4, ability cap ~7  (many Astropaths)\n  Alpha   — Talent 5, ability cap ~8  (Astartes Librarians, Black Ship candidates)\n  Alpha-Plus — Talent 6+, ability cap 9+ (beyond-play tier: Primarchs,\n    the most powerful Inquisitors, the worst Chaos sorcerers)\n\nLore: The Iota-to-Alpha-Plus assignment scale is drawn from the Adeptus Astra Telepathica's own grading of psychic potential as depicted across Dark Heresy-line material and repeated in Codex/Imperial Armour background; Black Library uses the same grades in passing (e.g. Eisenhorn-cycle and Ciaphas Cain references to Alpha-class talents). \"Alpha-Plus\" specifically denotes the rarest, most dangerous talents the Black Ships process.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting three things (two skills plus the Perils resistance roll) sits at Basic's \"Medium Talent\" rate of 10 points/level (B90, three-to-five-skill bracket) rather than 5 (one-two skills) or 15 (six-nine skills). Capped at level 6 for player characters; higher grades are GM-reference only, per the framework's \"Beyond play\" tier.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1,
			"base_points": 0,
			"max_levels": 6
		},
		{
			"id": "tHR86AagYD-Wkeb40",
			"name": "Witch-sight",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The first, mildest sign of the gift: an unlearned, passive prickling sense of daemonic taint, psychic activity, and warp corruption — how unsanctioned psykers are so often found, and how veteran Inquisitors and Space Marines notice a place has gone wrong before anything visible happens. Widely depicted; no single canonical source, consistent across Black Library and Dark Heresy-line material alike.\n\nDesign: Built as Detect (Psychic Activity & Daemonic Taint), an uncommon but not vanishingly rare category, base 10 per Basic's Detect cost table (B47-48). No Perils of the Warp: it is involuntary background sense, not a deliberate act of channelling.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mlcBJg0uzTYWi1xgM",
					"name": "Warp",
					"cost_adj": "-15%"
				}
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "Tg1NlCdB0H4Cdc11K",
			"name": "Unsanctioned Psyker",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Discovery of an unregistered psyker is, at best, summary transport to a Black Ship and Soul-binding or the Scholastia Psykana; at worst, on-the- spot execution by any Adepta Sororitas, Inquisitor, or panicking mob with a lasgun. This is one of the most consistent beats in Black Library's Imperium (Eisenhorn's witch-hunts; the fate of countless minor characters across the Gaunt's Ghosts and Ciaphas Cain series) and Dark Heresy's default assumption for any PC psyker who hasn't been Sanctioned.\n\nDesign: A single Secret (Basic B94-95) covers it: discovery is not rare (any strong emotional moment or unlucky witness can trigger it) and its consequences are severe and often fatal, so it sits at -20 points, toward the harsh end of Secret's usual range.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Social",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t2BzuZkw6UVRNYpth",
					"name": "Secret (Unsanctioned Psyker)",
					"reference": "B94",
					"local_notes": "Exposure means the Black Ships, summary execution, or worse.\n\nLore: See parent trait.\n\nDesign: See parent trait.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Social",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"base_points": -20
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TcWeYUUQdRA0G0A4P",
			"name": "Sanctioned Psyker",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Sanctioned psyker has passed through the Scholastia Psykana or an equivalent rite, is legally registered, and typically serves the Astra Militarum, an Inquisitor, or their Chapter's Librarius. They are watched, bound by oath and duty, and — even legally protected — are still called \"witch\" behind their back by the citizens they defend. Consistent across Dark Heresy/Only War's Sanctioned-psyker rules and Black Library's depiction of Primaris Psykers and Astartes Librarians alike.\n\nDesign: Duty to the Astra Telepathica/Inquisition (Basic B133 Duty table, hazardous, 12 or less) at -10, plus a mild, legally-protected Social Stigma (\"sanctioned witch,\" -5, lighter than Basic's Minority Group tier because the law is nominally on their side). Net -15.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Social",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
