		{
			"id": "TiHlJY8ogfOvJmJ2u",
			"name": "Navigator's Third Eye",
			"local_notes": "Lore: House Navigators carry a third eye, fused shut beneath the brow, that perceives the Astronomican's light and the safe currents of the warp well enough to steer a ship through it — and that kills or maddens anyone it meets uncovered. Navigators keep the eye bound at all times except when navigating from a shielded bridge. The clearest published detail on this comes from the Rogue Trader corebook's Navigator background rather than Black Library, which mostly gestures at the Navis Nobilite and the \"third eye\" in passing (e.g. Horus Heresy-era references); treat the RPG-tier material as the calibration source here per the framework's source-priority rule. Over centuries of psychic strain, aged Navigators are widely depicted as growing increasingly strange or deformed (a GM-invoked cosmetic/social disadvantage for very old Navigators — e.g. escalating Unnatural Features — rather than a fixed cost baked into this trait).\n\nDesign: Deliberately carries no Warp or Perils of the Warp modifier: the third eye is an always-on inherited mutation, not a deliberately channelled act — though a GM is free to call for a one-off Perils-style check during an extreme event like flying blind through a warp storm. Net for the package: 12 (Warp Sight) + 6 (Lethal Gaze) = 18.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium",
				"Navis Nobilite"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t68YzDrZWKcg4xl8O",
					"name": "Warp Sight",
					"local_notes": "Lore: What lets a Navigator see the Astronomican's beacon and the warp's safer currents across a voyage, letting a ship cross the immaterium without being lost or devoured.\n\nDesign: Detect (Basic B47-48), base 5 for an uncommon, specific thing; Precise doubles that (expressed here as +100%, equivalent to Basic's x2 multiplier); Increased Range at the highest tabled step (+60%, the \"100x\" tier) reflects effectively unlimited range while the eye is open in transit; Accessibility (-10%) reflects that it only works, and is only tolerable, while uncovered.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium",
						"Navis Nobilite"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mw-3ZVccJc_ucXv3a",
							"name": "Precise",
							"cost_adj": "+100%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mXIJ7Obt8DCk7YZ_9",
							"name": "Increased Range x100",
							"cost_adj": "+60%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mNqz8CYN_aReKeZ2o",
							"name": "Accessibility (only while the third eye is uncovered)",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 5,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "skill_bonus",
							"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Navigation"
							},
							"amount": 4,
							"specialization": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Warp"
							}
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tKBA9G7efGskpgCnM",
					"name": "Lethal Gaze",
					"local_notes": "Lore: This is why the eye stays bound: anyone who meets it directly, with no warding, risks death or madness. It is a narrative hazard for the people around a Navigator far more than a usable weapon for the player.\n\nDesign: Affliction (Basic B35), base 10, delivering a -50 point \"Mind-Sundering\" disadvantage (+50%); Sense-Based (Vision) (-20%, B112, only affects those who can see the eye); Uncontrollable (-30%, B113, the Navigator cannot pick and choose who is struck); Accessibility limiting it to moments the eye is uncovered (-10%).",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium",
						"Navis Nobilite"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mRCAbEkvELnGyOIGj",
							"name": "Disadvantage Delivered (Mind-Sundering",
							"local_notes": "An unshielded mortal who meets the open eye is killed, driven mad, or worse, GM's call; only other Navigators and comparably warp-touched beings routinely survive it.",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mYhFszpYiqLV4JUkk",
							"name": "Sense-Based (Vision)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mK_zGf19pKqB8I5XC",
							"name": "Uncontrollable",
							"cost_adj": "-30%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mIh5crKleullSdgwE",
							"name": "Accessibility (only when the third eye is uncovered)",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TcrlRstYZlaIlQIxR",
			"name": "Biomancy",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The discipline of the flesh: hardening it, mending it, driving it faster than it should go, or unmaking an enemy's body from the inside. Calibrated against Dark Heresy's Biomancy power list (Smite, Iron Arm, Warp Speed, Haemorrhage all appear there under close-to-these names) and Black Library depictions of Biomancer-Librarians and Chaos \"physicians.\"\n\nDesign: Talent-linked group; see each child for build math. All children take Warp (-15%) and Perils of the Warp (-10%) unless noted.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tfmwrnTbnQZqHGuX3",
					"name": "Smite",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A short-ranged bolt of biomantic force that ruptures vessels and boils blood.\n\nDesign: Innate Attack (Toxic, 4/die, B61) with the standard Warp/Perils pair, net -25%.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
